"Slide 1 (https://www.buffalolib.org/sites/default/files/getgraphic/resources/HowtoReadManga.pdf)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 11; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.buffalolib.org/sites/default/files/getgraphic/resources/HowtoReadManga.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 71\nL0@P0: Some Manga Basics\nL1@P0: • Manga is the Japanese word for comics-literally\nL2@P0: translated it means “whimsical pictures”\nL3@P0: • In it’s modern form, Manga dates from shortly\nL4@P0: after WWII.\nL5@P0: • In Japan, Manga is read by people of all ages\nL6@P0-1: and covers a wide range of topics.More Manga Info.\nL7@P1: • Like graphic novels, Manga uses text and\nL8@P1: pictures to present information\nL9@P1: • Traditionally, Manga is read from back-to-front,\nL10@P1: with panels and dialog balloons read right-to-left\nL11@P1: • When produced for the U.S. market, some\nL12@P1-2: Manga is “flipped” so that it can be read front-to\u0002back and left-to-right.Parts of a Manga Book\nL13@P2: Manga consists of:\nL14@P2-3: cover and inside pagesA Manga Page\nL15@P3: Pages consist of a variety of elements\nL16@P3: • Panels-squares or rectangles that contain a single scene\nL17@P3: • Gutters-space between panels\nL18@P3: • Dialog Balloons-contain communication between/among characters\nL19@P3: • Thought Balloons-contain a character’s thoughts\nL20@P3: • Captions-contain information about a scene or character\nL21@P3-4: • Sound Effects-visual sound clues i.e.. Wonk! Pow!Thought\nL22@P4: Balloon\nL23@P4: Sound Effect\nL24@P4: Panel\nL25@P4: Gutter\nL26@P4-5: Dialog BalloonCaptionHow to read a Manga Page\nL27@P5: Unlike traditional western texts, Manga is read from back-to-front\nL28@P5: Often, if you start at what looks like the front of the Manga, you will see a message like this It says: “You’re\nL29@P5: reading the wrong way!\nL30@P5-6: You should then flip to what looks like the back of the Manga and begin therePanel Order\nL31@P6: the panels on each page are similarly “reversed” and read in a right-to\u0002left sequence\nL32@P6: 1\nL33@P6: 3\nL34@P6: 4\nL35@P6: 2\nL36@P6: 5\nL37@P6: 6\nL38@P6: 7\nL39@P6: 10\nL40@P6: 9\nL41@P6: 8\nL42@P6: 11\nL43@P6-7: Just as you read traditional Manga pages “reversed,” in a back-to-front sequence,Dialog\nL44@P7: Balloons\nL45@P7: Dialog balloons are\nL46@P7: read right-to-left as\nL47@P7: well.\nL48@P7: 1\nL49@P7: 3\nL50@P7: 4\nL51@P7: 2\nL52@P7: 8\nL53@P7: 5\nL54@P7: 6710\nL55@P7: 9\nL56@P7-8: 11• Some Americanized Manga has been “flipped”\nL57@P8: so that it reads front-to-back and left-to-right\nL58@P8: • Some newer Manga is written by Western\nL59@P8: writers and is created with a front-to-back, left\u0002to-right orientation\nL60@P8: • If you are unsure of which way to read your\nL61@P8-9: Manga, try a couple of pages and see if the storyline is making any sense!Further Reading\nL62@P9: For more information about Manga, take a look at\nL63@P9: these resources:\nL64@P9: • Shonen Jump – a monthly magazine put out by\nL65@P9: Viz Communications\nL66@P9: • Understanding Manga and Anime by Robin\nL67@P9: Brenner\nL68@P9: • Making Comics: Storytelling Secrets of Comics,\nL69@P9-10: Manga, and Graphic Novels by Scott McCloud• Librarian’s Guide to Anime & Manga\nL70@P10: • Tokyopop• Viz Communications\n"
